# Clean Code: Principles

## Follow standard conventions - use existing project patterns, don't invent new ones

```php
// BAD - inventing a new pattern when project uses Form Requests
public function store(Request $request): void {
    $validated = $request->validate([...]);
}

// GOOD - follow the existing convention
public function store(StoreOrderRequest $request): void {
    $validated = $request->validated();
}
```

## KISS - Simpler is always better

```php
// BAD
$result = ($condition === true) ? (($value !== null) ? $value : $default) : $fallback;

// GOOD
if ($condition) {
    $result = $value ?? $default;
} else {
    $result = $fallback;
}
```

## Boy Scout Rule - Leave code cleaner than you found it

When touching a file, fix small issues you see: unclear names, dead code, missing types.

## Always find root cause - Don't patch symptoms

```php
// BAD - patching the symptom
try {
    $user->charge($amount);
} catch (\Exception $e) {
    $user->charge($amount); // just retry blindly
}

// GOOD - investigate and handle the root cause
if (!$user->hasValidPaymentMethod()) {
    throw new PaymentMethodRequiredException();
}
$user->charge($amount);
```

## Code Smell Awareness

| Smell | Sign | Fix |
|-------|------|-----|
| **Rigidity** | Small change cascades everywhere | Decouple with interfaces/DI |
| **Fragility** | Unrelated things break | Improve encapsulation |
| **Immobility** | Can't reuse code elsewhere | Extract standalone modules |
| **Needless Complexity** | Built for hypothetical futures | Apply YAGNI |
| **Needless Repetition** | Copy-paste code | Extract shared logic |
| **Opacity** | Hard to understand | Rename, simplify, split |
